  "worked_solution": "From the hyperboloid equation, we make $z$ the subject as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{16} & =-1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}+1 & =\\frac{{z}^{2}}{16}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{z}^{2}}{16} & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}+1 \\\\ {z}^{2} & =16(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}+1) \\\\ z & =\\pm 4\\sqrt{\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe square root is defined only for non-negative arguments. However, notice that for all $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}+1\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of $z(x,y)$ is ${R}^{2}.$",
